eal clinical scheduling [Data: Entities (3281, 286); Relationships (6496, 300)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"False positives and normal variants are expected: inflammation/infection and physiologic uptake can mimic malignancy\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community repeatedly emphasizes interpretive pitfalls: increased uptake is not unique to cancer, and inflammation or infection can create bright spots that may be confused with tumor activity [Data: Entities (3768, 212, 1570); Relationships (7789)]. This highlights a key reputational/quality point of PET interpretation—high sensitivity to biological activity, but limited specificity without context [Data: Entities (212, 471)].\\n\\nNormal tissues can also appear bright. Tonsil lymphoid tissue is explicitly called out as an example of normal PET brightness that must be interpreted cautiously, reinforcing that not all bright findings indicate disease [Data: Entities (1556); Relationships (2539)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"PET imaging is often paired with CT or MRI to improve localization and interpretation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"PET imaging is described as a method that measures tracer uptake and displays higher accumulation as brighter areas; it is frequently combined with other modalities (commonly CT or MRI) to add anatomical context and improve precision of localization [Data: Entities (302, 212)]. The PET/MRI scan is a specific combined method in this community, underscoring a technical capability to integrate functional (PET) and structural (MRI) information [Data: Entities (3283); Relationships (6944)].\\n\\nThis combination theme supports a general analytic point: bright spots have interpretive value, but anatomical correlation is important to understand what structure the uptake corresponds to and to reduce misinterpretation [Data: Entities (302, 212, 1570)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Primary clinical uses include assessing extent/spread and monitoring treatment 